  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1107 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nA broad upper-level trough over the central Rockies with several\nembedded disturbances continues to lift northeast towards our\narea. This is resulting in widespread showers and a few\nthunderstorms across eastern Kansas and eastern Nebraska. This\nrain will continue to lift northeast overnight into Saturday\nmorning. It'll be a soggy start to the weekend with 1 to 2\ninches of rainfall for many. Temperatures hold steady in the 60s\nduring this time. \n\nIn the wake of the morning rain, a brief period of drier\nconditions may develop, though spotty showers could linger. \nTemperatures should warm into the upper 60s to perhaps low 70s \nby mid-afternoon. As we move through the afternoon and evening \nanother round of rainfall is forecast. While the ascent from the\nupper-level trough will be positioned east of the area, \nlingering ascent under this disturbance should help with \nredevelopment. The best chance (50-70%) of showers and \nthunderstorms is expected across far eastern Nebraska and \nwestern Iowa heading into Saturday night into Sunday morning. \nWhile rain gradually shifts east through Sunday morning, patchy\nfog is likely to develop behind it. \n\nFog should lift by Sunday afternoon as drier conditions return \nto the region. While it remains mostly cloudy, most locations \nshould see 70s for highs by the afternoon. Dry weather largely \ncontinues into Monday with only a low chance of showers Monday \nmorning. The clouds linger, but another dry afternoon in the 70s\ncan be expected. \n\nThe dry weather does not last long. The remnants of Polo will \ncombine with an upper-level trough approaching the area heading \ninto the work week. By early Tuesday morning, widespread \nshowers and thunderstorms begin to push into the area once \nagain. Periodic showers and thunderstorms may linger into much \nof the remainder of the week as this system will be slow moving.\nThat said, uncertainty remains on how quickly this system \nprogresses across the region. That said, with the abundance of \nrecent rainfall, flash flooding and rises in area rivers may \nbecome an increasing concern. Outside the return of wet weather,\ncool temperatures in the 60s make a return as well.",
